From 0a532cd76ddfaf488232431eaf4c12865f4ac52c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Leidinger <Alexander@Leidinger.net>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 19:16:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/17] libclamav: store AC transition rows as 32-bit node
 indices

Each internal Aho-Corasick node owns a dense 256-entry transition row.
Holding node pointers makes that 2048 bytes on a 64-bit target; holding
indices into a node table makes it 1024.

Rows are built in the indexed form rather than converted afterwards, and
ac_build_nodeidx() fills the index table once at the end of
cli_ac_buildtrie() so the scan loop stays a single unconditional lookup.

The index scheme is 0 = unset, 1 = ac_root, i + 2 = ac_nodetable[i].
Index 0 is deliberately not ac_root: ac_maketrans() ends its fail-walk
on !fail->trans[i] and ac_root->fail is NULL, so ac_root must be a
truthy index.

Measured on a 424 MB signature set (3,849,249 signatures), resident
memory after cl_engine_compile(): 1,135,468 KB -> 1,059,800 KB,
-75,668 KB (-6.7%).

Scan output is unchanged over a 2,400-file tree, with an EICAR control
detected by both builds. Scan time is unchanged within measurement
error; loading is about 1.1x faster.

The saving is specific to 64-bit targets. On a 32-bit target the rows do
not shrink and the index table is a small net cost.

(cherry picked from commit 9e5d37b5d5da5a6f6ace73052d003d53011ea1a7)
