From cb5822bf3d261a7710667002dc0aa138b7aec522 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Leidinger <Alexander@Leidinger.net>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 19:16:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 02/17] libclamav: split the rarely-used tail off struct
 cli_ac_patt

struct cli_ac_patt is 136 bytes and most of it is inert for an ordinary
pattern. Measured over 1,713,408 patterns, 41,134 (2.40%) use any of
customdata, special, special_table, mindist, maxdist, boundary, ch,
ch_mindist or ch_maxdist.

Move those to struct cli_ac_patt_ext behind a pointer that stays NULL
for the rest, taking the struct to 96 bytes. Field order still avoids
interior padding, per 9381324ad (bb#748).

The AC_EXT_* accessors return what the inline field held when the
feature was unused, so patt_cmp_fn() compares identically.

cli_ac_addsig() parses into a stack extension and allocates one only if
it ends up used. Allocating per pattern and freeing the empty ones was
tried first and is worse under mpool, whose free lists are keyed by size
class: a 48-byte extension shares a class with the AC pattern arrays,
which are retained, so each freed extension is reused by one of those
and the arena grows once per pattern.

Measured on a 424 MB signature set (3,849,249 signatures), resident
memory after cl_engine_compile(): 1,135,468 KB -> 1,058,872 KB,
-76,596 KB (-6.7%).

Scan output is unchanged over a 2,400-file tree, with an EICAR control
detected by both builds.

(cherry picked from commit 2ad7b6513d2d1bd5c7941f6e0219554adc6bc202)
