+# Memory optimization literature update
+
+Search completed 28 October 2026 UTC. Scope: the [actual algorithms and allocations](memory-algorithm-inventory.md) in this fork, including the surrounding load/verify/free paths. This is a targeted primary-source review, not an exhaustive systematic review of all literature. “New” below means newly reviewed for this project, not necessarily newly invented. A primary abstract or implementation read is explicitly distinguished from examination of the paper text.
+
+## Decisions
+
+Experimentally implemented exact immutable logical-metadata sharing: parser-owned objects become matcher-owned shared objects; the scan still reads ordinary pointers and evaluates the original expression. The existing hash-table implementation supplies collision handling and full equality. This shifts work/ownership to loading, while adding a small loader module and a uniqueness-dependent memory cost; it is not a claim of fewer lines or invariants overall.
+
+**Acceptance result:** retain the implementation as an experimental patch, not in the active scanner. A repeated duplicate-pattern fixture changes all-match order because existing sort ties use allocation addresses. Ordinary corpus equality and strong-threat selection are insufficient to pass the stricter order gate. See [the implementation report](logical-sharing-implementation.md).
+
+The best next investigations under the complexity constraint are (1) prove field dependencies and remove redundant stored values, (2) count exact ordered candidate suffix sharing before changing traversal, and (3) account for region lifetimes and allocation rounding before trying a new allocator. They have plausible paths to replacing existing machinery. Broad learned codecs, moving-object runtimes, decoder caches and custom compression hardware do not qualify for integration in this pass.
+
+## Newly reviewed and recent primary work
+
+| Work / publication status | What was examined | Concrete connection and decision |
+|---|---|---|
+| **[Practical Bit Vectors Supporting Constant Time Rank and Select in Optimal Space](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2026.8)** — Kurpicz, Rigi-Luperti, Sanders; ESA, 25 Aug 2026 | Paper text, block/summary design and evaluation; [implementation](https://codeberg.org/limnopilos/limnopilos). | Worst-case constant-time rank/select with low redundancy makes large monotone directories more interesting. Paper's below-1% overhead and difficult-input speed results concern bitvectors, not whole ClamAV memory. Evaluation includes very large vectors; per-small-block metadata and sparse extremes can defeat the transfer. Test global offset directories first, not a separate succinct tree for every tiny exception mask. |
+| **[Efficient Symbolic Computation via Hash Consing](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.20534v1)** — Zhu et al.; 24 Sep 2025 preprint, marked under review | Paper architecture and evaluation discussion. | Supports examining immutable duplication, but not its Julia weak-reference/global-GC architecture. Some reported workloads increase memory, emphasizing interner overhead. Our implementation uses engine lifetime, exact bytes and original expressions; no DAG normalization, memoization or cross-engine ownership. The technique itself is longstanding. |
+| **[Reconsidering “Reconsidering Custom Memory Allocation”](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.17119v1)** — van Kempen, Berger; ISMM, Jun 2026, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/3814942.3816132) | Paper text, region/locality evaluation and scope. | Lifetime-separated regions and fragmented-heap experiments are relevant to construction/reload. This is not evidence for a numerical memory reduction: the paper explicitly does not establish footprint savings and regions can retain more memory. Prefer measurement and ownership cleanup over an unqualified global allocator swap. |
+| **[OBASE: Object-Based Address-Space Engineering to Improve Memory Tiering](https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi26/presentation/banakar)** — OSDI, Jul 2026; [updated preprint](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00378) | Primary abstract and updated version metadata. | Object/page locality matters when hot and cold fields share pages. Runtime instrumentation, migration and address-space engineering add substantial machinery and interact with stable name pointers. Borrow only static placement by known role/lifetime; do not import its reported improvements as ClamAV estimates. |
+| **[Engineering faster double-array Aho–Corasick automata](https://doi.org/10.1002/spe.3190)** — Kanda, Akabe, Oda; SPE 2023, [author text updated Jun 2024](https://arxiv.org/html/2207.13870v3) | Full output-representation sections, construction and evaluation; [Daachorse](https://github.com/daac-tools/daachorse). | Shared failure-output suffixes and output forests target the 69.578 MiB candidate allocation. Count identical ordered tails, not sets; preserve duplicates, severity and `next_same`. Forests trade contiguous iteration for dependent accesses. Compact double-array state sizes exclude ClamAV's extended verifier/metadata; replacing the entire automaton is a separate, more complex project. |
+| **[Corra: Correlation-Aware Column Compression](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.17229v2)** — CloudDB/VLDB workshop, 2024 | Paper text and horizontal/delta/dictionary schemes. | The important question is whether metadata fields determine one another. A proven dependency may allow field removal with less representation complexity. Conditional correlations require selectors/residuals and can add complexity. Start with source-proven offset and identifier dependencies, then test every exported record and malformed/custom signatures. No database benchmark ratio is used as a ClamAV forecast. |
+| **[The FastLanes File Format](https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/35881)** — Afroozeh, Boncz; PVLDB 18(11), 2025 | Primary publication record and implementation/design descriptions. | Lightweight cascades and correlated columns fit metadata analysis. Batch SIMD decoding does not imply fast single-record candidate access. Treat the existing constant/affine/FOR prototypes as the comparison baseline; any replacement must remove their machinery, not layer another codec family on top. |
+| **[F3: The Open-Source Data File Format for the Future](https://db.cs.cmu.edu/papers/2025/zeng-sigmod2025.pdf)** — Zeng et al.; PACMMOD, Sep 2025, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/3749163) | Primary paper abstract/design introduction. | Fine-grained layouts are relevant to immutable compiled data. Embedded WebAssembly decoders add executable-format validation, runtime and trust obligations; excluded. Static layout specialization is the transferable idea. |
+| **[BtrBlocks: Efficient Columnar Compression for Data Lakes](https://www.cs.cit.tum.de/fileadmin/w00cfj/dis/papers/btrblocks.pdf)** — SIGMOD 2023 | Primary paper abstract and implementation description. | Adaptive lightweight compression is useful prior art for scalar columns, not a newly discovered universal improvement. Selection metadata, decode granularity and exceptions must be included. Not integrated as another compression subsystem. |
+| **[LICO: An SIMD-Aware High-Performance Learned Inverted Index Compression Framework](https://doi.org/10.1145/3802079)** — SIGMOD/PACMMOD 2026; [official repository](https://github.com/xianyuzhuruc/LICO) | Publication metadata and implementation formats; full technical PDF not reliably extracted in this pass. | Expects increasing integer postings and offers SIMD variants. Monotone offsets might fit; candidate order generally does not. Sorting alerts to fit the codec is unacceptable. Model/residual decoding adds a runtime path; research-only until it can replace an existing one and win single-lookup latency. |
