+| **[Optimal Static Fully Indexable Dictionaries](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol334-icalp2025/html/LIPIcs.ICALP.2025.114/LIPIcs.ICALP.2025.114.html)** — ICALP 2025 | Primary theorem/abstract. | Near-entropy representations motivate monotone and bitmap directories. Universe assumptions and query-time parameters matter; this is not a production-ready constant-cost replacement for all offset arrays. Quantify the index fraction first. |
+| **[Compressed Dictionary Matching on Run-Length Encoded Strings](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00453-026-01398-0)** — Bille et al.; Algorithmica, 28 May 2026, extending CPM 2025 | Paper introduction, theorem and model. | Complexity in pattern/text run counts is attractive only with substantial runs and the appropriate exact-string model. Binary files, tagged tokens and short AC anchors do not automatically fit. Encoding the incoming text and occurrence output remain costs. Measure run distributions before proposing an engine rewrite. |
+| **[Incongruity-Sensitive Access to Highly Compressed Strings](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2026.125)** — Cicalese et al.; ESA, 25 Aug 2026 | Primary abstract and access-bound formulation. | Grammar/block-tree access sensitive to local repetitiveness may improve token-store probes. It is not uniform one-step access; repeated/adversarial regions still matter. A replacement for an existing grammar accessor is conceivable, but a new engine grammar decoder is outside this pass. |
+| **[Logarithmic-Time Internal Pattern Matching Queries in Compressed and Dynamic Texts](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00224-026-10266-x)** — Theory of Computing Systems, 2026 | Primary abstract/model. | Internal substring queries on a compressed text differ from external arbitrary signatures with ClamAV extensions. Useful theoretical boundary, low direct integration fit. |
+| **[Succinct Data Structures for Segments](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CPM.2025.27)** — Bille, Gørtz, Tarnow; CPM 2025 | Primary abstract. | Segment rank queries illustrate succinct geometric indexing, but are not the operations performed by flat candidate links. No direct saving claimed. |
+| **[Towards Efficient Matching of Regexes with Backreferences using Register Set Automata](https://www.fit.vut.cz/research/result/c211925/)** — Havlena et al.; PLDI 2026, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/3808281) | Institutional publication/abstract and supported class. | Restricted backreference classes can admit better matching algorithms; this does not preserve all PCRE2 semantics. Measure regex memory first. Any selective replacement adds routing and semantic qualification. |
+| **[From regular expressions to deterministic finite automata: … states necessary and sufficient](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20555)** — Martynova, Okhotin; Apr 2025 preprint | Primary abstract/lower-bound statement. | Exponential state blow-up remains possible even over a binary alphabet. “Compile every regex to a DFA” is therefore not a memory-safe general strategy; PCRE's extra features further restrict equivalence. |
+| **[Ribbon: Fast Succinct Static Retrieval and Approximate Membership](https://doi.org/10.1145/3785417)** — JACM, Feb 2026; [institutional paper](https://publikationen.bibliothek.kit.edu/1000191446/177619178) | Publication metadata and primary abstract. | Retrieval/approximate membership can be a prefilter, followed by exact verification. It cannot replace exact digest checks. Existing q-gram filters total only 0.5 MiB; adding a new filter is unlikely to solve the dominant memory problem. |
+| **[Random-Access Hardware Sequence Compression](https://github.com/HEAP-Lab-VT/rst)** — ISCA 2026; [artifact](https://zenodo.org/records/19493662) | Official implementation/artifact description. | Bounded local dictionaries and random block access are interesting, but hardware evaluation is not evidence of a deployable SiteGround capability. A software emulation requires its own cost model. Excluded from this fork. |
+| **[COMPAT: A Compression Framework with Fine-Grain and Object-Level Compression Units](https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/553007)** — IPDPS 2026, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1109/IPDPS65963.2026.00045) | Institutional publication/abstract. | Object-granular compression hardware may avoid page-granularity waste, but requires capabilities outside a portable scanner. No hosting memory estimate uses it. |
+| **[Efficient pattern matching in compressed text via bit-parallel word-based encoding](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2773186326000101)** — Franklin Open, Mar 2026 | Publisher abstract. | Word-based encoding and bit-parallel filtering address a different input model. Exact verification is still necessary; arbitrary binary normalization/tagged signatures prevent a drop-in substitution. |
+
+## Previously reviewed work retained as baselines
+
+The earlier documents contain the source links and experimental details for OnPair (including the 2026 implementation update), OptFSST (2026), Orzo/Elias–Fano, PtrHash, optimal static dictionaries, grammar random access and LZBE, bit-packed grammars, C²trie, Mesh, initializable arrays and PGM. These were not counted as newly discovered work. The newest date alone does not determine priority: the older AC output-sharing and Corra papers fit the actual allocations more closely than several 2026 hardware or compressed-text results.
+
+## Theory-to-practice tests
+
+1. **Eliminate information before encoding it.** If a field is a total function of retained fields for every accepted signature, storing it is redundant. Establish the invariant in parser and verifier code, exercise integer bounds and malformed input, then remove the field and its ownership/access code. A correlation observed only in the current database is insufficient. This is the strongest prospective complexity reduction.
+2. **Share ordered tails, not sets.** Count equal candidate suffixes including multiplicity, order, `next_same` and all record attributes. A shared immutable suffix can reduce allocation without a decompressor. Estimate savings after directories and indices. Reject if maintaining the original order requires per-scan sorting or a merge heap.
+3. **Use the index-size denominator.** A 50% reduction of a 1 MiB directory saves 0.5 MiB, not half the dataset. Rank/select and MPH improvements should be charged only to the index they replace, with full keys and payload retained. Large-bitvector results need small-array crossover tests.
+4. **Separate logical size from resident size.** Sharing can reduce allocated payload while a pool retains pages. Measure compiled RSS, construction high-water, post-scan memory, old/new reload overlap and cgroup usage. Compare lifetime-separated regions only after attributing the unexplained allocation budget.
+5. **Stress dependent accesses.** Candidate floods, repetitive byte streams, random misses, high-output inputs and cold pages reveal costs hidden by sequential codec benchmarks. Include full ordered output comparisons, not just equal match counts.
+6. **Treat arbitrary valid custom databases as the worst case.** An exact interner can save nothing and add its keys/index. Random-like digest keys and unique metadata cannot be promised compression. A conservative worst-case bound is part of the design, not an optional benchmark.
+
+## Immediate leads revisited
+
+- L23 implemented, then gated out: shared descriptor/expression ownership saves memory and needs no scan-time table lookup, but changes allocation-dependent ordering.
+- L24 adjacent-run sharing: simpler than global interning, but less sharing; prior run counts depended on the flawed export and are withdrawn until recomputed.
+- L25 allocator gap: census attribution remains necessary; no “all fragmentation” estimate.
+- L26 byte-only token specialization: substantial potential, but another verifier representation/path; not integrated under the current gate.
+- L27 range-count defect: corrected; prior logical template estimates and derived totals are superseded.
+- L28 rank/select: strongest for large monotone directories, weak for tiny exception masks.
+- L29 OBASE: borrow static lifetime/role grouping, reject runtime movement here.
+- L30 output forests: worth an exact ordered-tail census, not a wholesale automaton replacement.
+- L31 interner overhead: explicit all-distinct regression bound and duplicate-dependent break-even; no universal saving claim.
+- L32 ordering gate: repeated strict-order failure, preserve the default representation.
+- L33 post-link compaction: investigate reusing an existing relocation phase only; pool residency and ownership remain unresolved.
+
+No paper establishes that this fork can reach 202.042 MiB while preserving its current end-to-end performance. The measurements in the implementation report are the evidence for this pass; literature results are leads and constraints, not substitute measurements.
